<TopicPage slug="subjects/psychology/advanced/neuropsychology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Imaging tells you when regions are busy; it cannot tell you what they are <em>for</em>. Only
    subtraction does that: remove a system and see what vanishes. Neuropsychology is the discipline
    of human brain lesions — strokes, tumours, trauma, degeneration treated as natural experiments —
    and it remains the most causal evidence available about human cognition, because ethics forbids
    the experimental alternative. Its other half is applied: standardised assessment that turns
    patterns of preserved and impaired function into diagnoses, rehabilitation plans and
    capacity decisions.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the lesion logic, the famous syndromes, and recovery science" widget="equationStep" config={neuroCfg} />

  <h2>The lesion method and its logic</h2>
  <p>
    The founding cases set the template. Broca's patient "Tan" (1861) — named for his only spoken
    syllable — understood language and produced almost none; autopsy revealed a left inferior
    frontal lesion, and Broca accumulated further cases ("we speak with the left hemisphere," he
    concluded). Wernicke (1874) found the mirror patient: fluent, rapid, largely meaningless speech
    with comprehension devastated, lesion in the left superior temporal region. Together they are
    the classic <strong>double dissociation</strong>: production damaged with comprehension spared,
    and vice versa — proof that language decomposes into independently supported systems. (Modern
    aphasiology has complicated the tidy two-area story — recovery profiles and imaging show a
    distributed left-perisylvian network — but the dissociation logic survives untouched.)
  </p>
  <p>
    The method's caveats are as important as its power. Lesions are uncontrolled in size, cause and
    chronicity; the brain reorganises after injury, so chronic deficits understate the acute
    contribution; and "damage to region R abolishes function X" need not mean R computes X — it may
    mean R is a necessary cable in a network that computes X elsewhere (connectome-style lesion
    modelling now tests which regions' disconnection best predicts which deficits). The discipline's
    mature form treats lesions as perturbations within networks, not map-pins on phrenological
    charts — the exact opposite of the 19th-century faculty psychology Broca's era is often
    confused with.
  </p>

  <h2>Frontal lobes: intelligence without judgement</h2>
  <p>
    Phineas Gage (1848) is the entry case — iron rod through the left frontal lobe, survival,
    and reported personality transformation ("Gage was no longer Gage"). The case's modern value
    comes from the patients Damasio and colleagues studied a century later: individuals with
    ventromedial prefrontal damage who have normal IQ, normal memory, and catastrophic real-world
    decision-making. The <strong>Iowa Gambling Task</strong> formalised the deficit: choosing from
    decks that are advantageous in the long run versus disastrous but occasionally lucrative,
    vmPFC patients keep drawing from the bad decks — even while their skin-conductance responses to
    the bad decks are absent (healthy controls show anticipatory "gut" responses before the losses
    register consciously). The <strong>somatic-marker hypothesis</strong> reads this as emotion
    carrying the load in value-based choice: strip the affective tags from options and decision-
    making collapses into endless analysis. The clinical dissociation — tested IQ intact, life
    functioning destroyed — is why executive assessment exists at all: Trail Making, Stroop,
    verbal fluency and the Wisconsin Card Sorting test (perseveration on a rule that has silently
    changed is the frontal signature) measure the systems IQ was never designed to touch.
  </p>

  <h2>Syndromes as windows: the dissociation catalogue</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Prosopagnosia:</strong> acquired (occipitotemporal/fusiform damage) or developmental
    face blindness — faces unrecognisable, often including one's own in a mirror, while other object
    recognition and all visual perception survive. The dissociation (faces lost, objects spared)
    argues for face-specialised processing; the reverse dissociation exists too, which is how
    category-specific systems were established.</li>
    <li><strong>Hemispatial neglect:</strong> after right parietal damage, patients ignore the left
    half of space — eating from one side of the plate, copying half a clock face, denying the
    neglected limb belongs to them in severe forms. The deep finding: neglect is not blindness
    (visual input reaches cortex) but a failure of attentional orientation — the mind's map of
    space, not the eye's.</li>
    <li><strong>Blindsight:</strong> V1-lesioned patients deny seeing in a scotoma yet above-chance
    discriminate location, motion and even emotional expressions there — subcortical routes
    (superior colliculus, pulvinar to amygdala) processing what visual awareness never receives.
    Consciousness research's cleanest dissociation between processing and awareness.</li>
    <li><strong>Capgras delusion:</strong> the conviction that family members are identical
    impostors. The two-route model: covert autonomic recognition survives (patients show normal
    skin-conductance response to familiar faces) while felt familiarity is lost — the mirror image
    of prosopagnosia, and a bridge case where neuropsychology meets belief formation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Assessment: profiles, not scores</h2>
  <p>
    Clinical practice runs on standardised batteries — WAIS (intelligence), WMS (memory), Boston
    (language), Trail Making and Wisconsin (executive), plus embedded and standalone
    <strong>effort/validity tests</strong> (poor effort is the largest corruptor of results, and
    malingering detection is a required competency). The diagnostic work is <strong>profile
    analysis</strong>: no single score localises anything, but the <em>pattern</em> does. Alzheimer's
    presents memory-first (hippocampal, per the memory-circuits lesson), vascular dementia steps
    down with each infarct, frontotemporal dementia opens with behaviour and language while memory
    holds — and each pattern carries different prognosis, treatment and family counselling. The same
    logic drives the subfield most people actually encounter: concussion protocols (baseline testing,
    graded return-to-play after sport-related brain injury), return-to-work and driving assessments
    after TBI, and capacity evaluation — whether a patient can understand, retain, weigh and
    communicate a decision, which is a legal determination informed by neuropsychological evidence.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "left-brained people are logical, right-brained people creative."
    The lateralisation literature supports modest hemispheric specialisations (language left-lateralised
    in ~95% of right-handers; right hemisphere stronger for spatial attention and prosody), but
    hemispheric-dominance personality claims fail every test of measurement: both hemispheres
    collaborate on essentially every real task, and the pop version descends from misread split-brain
    work. Related and equally dead: "we use 10% of our brains" (lesions anywhere cause deficits —
    the field's founding observation refutes it directly).
  </p>

  <h2>Plasticity and rehabilitation</h2>
  <p>
    The adult brain's recovery capacities are real and bounded. Post-stroke, spontaneous recovery
    is steepest in the first weeks to months, with deficits plateauing; therapy exploits the
    mechanism rather than waiting for it. <strong>Constraint-induced movement therapy</strong>
    restrains the unaffected arm for most waking hours and forces intensive use of the paretic one,
    reversing "learned non-use"; the EXCITE trial showed functional improvement roughly double
    usual care, with cortical representation of the affected limb expanding — structural evidence
    of reorganisation (the syllabus's Hölzel mindfulness study sits in this same tradition:
    training measurable in brain structure). Language therapy follows similar principles
    (intensity matters; timing matters), and mirror-box therapy for phantom-limb pain shows the
    visual system retraining the body schema. The limits are equally documented: regeneration of
    severed axons in the human CNS essentially does not occur, plasticity can be maladaptive
    (learned non-use itself, post-stroke pain, dystonia from maladaptive remapping), and
    rehabilitation is dose-dependent and expensive — the reason access, not biology, is often the
    binding constraint.
  </p>

  <h2>Aphasia and the language system, mapped</h2>
  <p>
    Language breakdown is the discipline's classic territory, and the classical syndromes
    remain clinically real even as the network view refines them. <strong>Broca's (non-fluent)
    aphasia</strong>: telegraphic, effortful output with largely preserved comprehension —
    production and syntax damaged. <strong>Wernicke's (fluent) aphasia</strong>: fluent,
    prosodic, empty or neologistic speech with poor comprehension — the patient characteristically
    unaware. <strong>Conduction aphasia</strong>: comprehension and production preserved,
    repetition devastated (the arcuate-fasciculus link) — the founding disconnection syndrome.
    <strong>Anomic aphasia</strong>: word-finding failure alone; <strong>global aphasia</strong>:
    everything, after large left-perisylvian lesions. The refined dual-stream model (a dorsal
    sensorimotor mapping stream, a ventral semantic-comprehension stream) predicts recovery
    patterns and guides therapy type; and <strong>alexia without agraphia</strong> — reading
    the same word one cannot write, from a lesion disconnecting visual input from left
    language cortex — remains the purest demonstration that reading is not one faculty.
    Recovery follows a predictable arc: early spontaneous gains, then a therapy-dependent
    plateau, with intensity and timing effects (earlier is better, massed practice helps)
    among the field's most practice-changing findings.
  </p>

  <h2>Amnesia's taxonomy: dissociating the systems</h2>
  <p>
    The amnesias refine the multiple-systems doctrine. <strong>Medial temporal amnesia</strong>
    (H.M.'s pattern) spares working memory and procedural learning.
    <strong>Diencephalic amnesia</strong> (Korsakoff's syndrome, from thiamine deficiency in
    alcoholism) damages the same declarative system through different hardware (mammillary
    bodies, dorsomedial thalamus), with confabulation as its signature complication.
    <strong>Transient global amnesia</strong> — hours-long anterograde failure resolving
    without trace, mechanism still debated — shows the system can fail transiently and
    completely. <strong>Functional (dissociative) amnesia</strong>, loss without structural
    lesion, often around psychological trauma, tests the boundary between hardware and
    software in memory. Each pattern is a natural double dissociation executed by disease,
    and the assessment batteries exist to place a patient within the taxonomy — because the
    diagnosis predicts prognosis, rehabilitation approach and, in Korsakoff's case,
    preventability.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The equipotentiality debate.</strong> Lashley's mass-action experiments (cortical
    extent, not location, predicted learning deficits in rats) contested strict localisation and
    still frames the field: functions are localised, but they are properties of networks whose
    lesion-effects depend on which nodes and which connections. Modern <strong>network lesion
    mapping</strong> (voxel-based lesion-symptom mapping combined with connectome data) resolves the
    old argument empirically: symptom prediction improves when damage is measured as disconnection
    of distributed circuits — Broca's area lesions cause expressive aphasia most reliably when they
    disconnect the underlying frontal-temporal pathway. <strong>Normative rigour.</strong>
    Interpretation depends on comparison samples: age, education and sex-adjusted norms, base rates
    of low scores in healthy people (a perfectly healthy adult typically scores below the 5th
    percentile on several subtests of a battery — the "abnormality" fallacy of single low scores),
    and the psychometric machinery from the previous lesson (reliability, SEM, validity) governs
    every clinical inference. <strong>Open questions:</strong> how much recovery after CNS injury is
    achievable, and whether stimulation (TMS, tDCS, spinal stimulation) plus training can push past
    the plateau; the emergence of chronic traumatic encephalopathy as a postmortem-only diagnosis
    and the absence of a living biomarker; how coma and disorders-of-consciousness assessment should
    handle covert awareness (fMRI command-following studies find it in a minority of
    behaviourally-unresponsive patients — a finding still working its way into ethics and law); and
    whether individualised lesion models can be made predictive enough to plan surgery by simulation
    before the scalpel moves — connectome medicine's stated goal.
  </p>
  <p>
    Assessment ethics and cross-cultural validity deserve explicit treatment, because the
    batteries are culture-laden instruments applied to diverse patients. Language tests assume
    language norms; education-dependent measures (reading, arithmetic, verbal fluency) confound
    schooling with ability; and norms collected on one population misclassify another — the
    documented pattern that makes cross-cultural neuropsychology a subfield of its own
    (adapted translations, education-corrected norms, nonverbal instruments for
    cross-linguistic use). The ethics layer raises the stakes: a capacity finding can remove a
    person's legal right to decide where they live or how their money is spent, so validity,
    cultural fairness and the assessor's own bias become directly consequential. Professional
    standards (consent for the assessment itself, feedback delivered usefully, results
    communicated with their error structure) exist because this subfield's numbers change
    lives more directly than most in psychology. Where norms are missing entirely, the honest
    report is qualitative description with explicit uncertainty — a discipline the field is
    still learning, and one that keeps measurement, ethics and law converging in the clinic
    more tightly than anywhere else in the discipline.
  </p>
  <p>
    The method's future points toward integration: neuropsychological profiles feeding
    computational models, lesion data constraining network models, and rehabilitation trials
    designed around the reorganisation biology rather than the impairment checklist. The
    discipline founded on what breaks is becoming the discipline of how the system
    reconfigures — and that shift, more than any single finding, is its contribution to the
    science of the injured brain.
  </p>

